\pard\tx1636\tx1996\pardeftab720\li1996\fi-1996\ri12\sa11\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0
\f1\b0\fs26 \cf3 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	1.	}\cf3 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Context and Objectives of the Study\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 Africa stands at a pivotal juncture in its development trajectory, where the convergence of demographic growth, urbanization, and climate vulnerability necessitates a radical reimagining of economic systems. The continent holds unparalleled potential to lead the global green transition, with renewable energy capacity estimated at 1,000 times its projected 2040 electricity demand, vast arable land for sustainable agriculture, and critical minerals essential for clean energy technologies. Yet this potential remains underleveraged: while Africa contributes less than 4% of global emissions (AfDB), it faces disproportionate climate impacts, including GDP losses projected at 20% by 2050 (Christian Aid / CPI ) due to warming trends.\par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 The African Development Bank (AfDB ) underscores that green growth models can simultaneously address energy poverty, food insecurity, and unemployment while safeguarding natural capital. For context, transitioning to renewable energy alone could boost Africa\'92s GDP by 6.4% by 2050 (IRENA), illustrating the transformative economic upside of climate-aligned industrialization.\
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 However, systemic barriers threaten to derail this promise. Africa\'92s annual climate finance gap now exceeds $230 billion, with current \uc0\u64258 ows covering just 23% of the funding required to meet 2030 climate goals (CPI / AfDB). Despite a 48% increase in climate finance to $44 billion in 2021/2022, private sector contributions remain stagnant at 18% of total \u64258 ows, and micro, small, and medium-sized enterprises (MSMEs ) face a $331 billion financing shortfall (CPI/UNDP). \
Policy fragmentation exacerbates these challenges: only 10% of climate finance originates domestically, and regulatory frameworks often lack harmonization across the African Union\'92s 54 member states. Green businesses also struggle to transition from venture capital to private equity due to mismatched valuation methodologies and limited investor confidence, leaving high-potential sectors like circular economy and green tech underfunded.\par
In this context, unlocking the potential of green enterprises requires more than just financing, it demands enabling these ventures to enter and scale across new markets. Regional expansion presents a powerful pathway for growth, allowing green entrepreneurs to build economies of scale, access new customers, and tap into complementary ecosystems. Yet this process is often constrained by fragmented regulations, underdeveloped support networks, and lack of investor coordination.\par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 It is against this backdrop that the IYBA-SEED Regional Expansion of SEEDs in Green Industries program, under Component 1 of the IYBA-SEED global initiative \'93Investing in Young Businesses in Africa \'96 Supporting Entrepreneurship Ecosystem Development, has launched a groundbreaking benchmark study. \
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 The study aims to identify market opportunities in green industries at national, regional, and pan-African levels, enabling entrepreneurs from South Africa, Kenya, Benin, Togo, and Senegal to scale their businesses strategically.\
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 The Green Industry Benchmark Study has been designed as both a research exercise and a strategic and pragmatic tool.\
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 The objectives of the Green Industry Benchmark Study are to:\

\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1295\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Identify high-opportunity green sub-sectors, prioritizing those with strong regional relevance, digital scalability, and inclusive growth potential.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1293\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Map demand and investment signals for green products and services across African regions, especially in adjacent or trade-linked markets.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1290\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Support ecosystem alignment, ensuring that national BDS providers, policy frameworks, and funding actors are better equipped to collaborate across borders.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1294\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Integrate gender and youth lenses to ensure that scaling pathways and opportunities are inclusive and equity driven.\par


\pard\tx1275\tx1678\pardeftab720\li1678\fi-1678\sa11\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl1
\f3\b \cf4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	1.1.	}
\f0 \cf4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Methodology and Approach\cf2 \par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1288\sa159\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 In this study, we use the term \'91green industries\'92 to refer to all economic sectors and industrial activities that incorporate environmental protection, reduction of ecological impacts and sustainable management of natural resources into their operations, production processes and value chains, such as: Renewable energies (e.g. solar mini-grids, productive solar kits, biomass), Waste management (e.g. recycling, composting, upcycling, e-waste), Water and sanitation (e.g. drinking water access technology, treatment, eco-friendly toilets), Sustainable agriculture (e.g. organic inputs, water-e
cient irrigation, green agriTech), Eco-construction (e.g. local materials, passive buildings, natural insulation), Clean mobility (e.g. electric vehicles, shared transport, green logistics), Circular economy (e.g.
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 recycled products, reuse, equipment rental), Green tech & digital (e.g. environmental monitoring tools, green fintech, eco-friendly education).\
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 Regional expansion refers to a company developing its business in a new region/county (e.g. for Kenya ) and/or a new country.\

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 To provide a comprehensive analysis of the green industry, sector by sector and at multiple levels ranging from global and continental to the 5 case study countries, this benchmark study has leveraged a mixed-method approach of data collection, analysis and reporting. A mixed-method approach to research involves the systematic integration of both qualitative and quantitative methods within a single study to provide a more comprehensive understanding of the research problem. This approach combines the numerical rigor of quantitative data (e.g., surveys, statistical analysis ) with the contextual depth of qualitative data (e.g., interviews, focus groups, document reviews). This triangulation enhances the credibility and richness of findings, particularly in complex fields like green economy policy or green businesses ecosystems, where both measurable trends and lived experiences are crucial. Through the triangulation of data from a quantitative literature review of published reports, studies and publications on the green industry in Africa and responses from qualitative interviews with targeted green industry experts in African and aboard, the benchmark study will ensure a well-rounded and actionable report.\
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 A systematic review was conducted of over 300 desktop research sources published between 2021 and 2025, spanning global, continental, national, and sector-specific perspectives. All sources were published from 2021 to ensure the relevance and accuracy of the findings. The published desktop sources consisted of the following (all the sources are listed in annexe 7.3):\
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1296\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls10\ilvl0
\f5\fs22\fsmilli11000 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f0\b\fs24 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Global/Continental Sources: 
\f1\b0 This included analyses of the green economy from reports published by DFIs like World Bank, the African Development Bank, the African Union, etc
\fs22 \par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1289\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls10\ilvl0
\f5\fs22\fsmilli11000 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f0\b\fs24 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone National Sources: 
\f1\b0 Key national policy documents were examined, such as Kenya\'92s Climate Act 2023, South Africa\'92s Just Energy Transition Partnership (JETP ) implementation plans, and Togo\'92s Circular Economy Act 2024, to understand national priorities and regulatory frameworks.
\fs22 \par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1295\sa159\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls10\ilvl0
\f5\fs22\fsmilli11000 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f0\b\fs24 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Sector-Specific Sources: 
\f1\b0 This category included reports and studies on climate finance, renewable energy, climate-tech, circular economy, carbon
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 markets, e-mobility, and agriculture, providing insights into specific sectors and financing trends.\
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1295\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls11\ilvl0
\f5\fs22\fsmilli11000 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f0\b\fs24 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Peer-viewed journals: 
\f1\b0 On the green economy and green industries of the world and Africa.
\fs22 \par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1288\sa96\qj\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf2 Complementing the desk research, qualitative and quantitative interviews were conducted with a diverse group of stakeholders comprising 20 experts in the green industry sector. These stakeholders included government representatives, development finance institutions, private investors, entrepreneurs, and technical advisors working within the green economy and financing sectors across the target countries and internationally. The structured interviews were conducted between April and May 2025 with 20 stakeholders across IYBA-SEED\'92s five target countries consisting of Benin, Kenya, Senegal, South Africa, Togo, as well as intercontinental (pan-African ) and international experts for a global perspective on the best practices in business models, expansions, policy and investments.\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 Stakeholder selection was strategically planned to ensure comprehensive representation across the five case study countries, with three experts on the green economy interviewed from each target country (South Africa, Kenya, Togo, Benin, and Senegal), and five selected internationally to incorporate global perspectives on innovation transfer, market access, and cross-border investment dynamics. This approach ensured the study captured both local nuances and international best practices in the green economy and trade, while maintaining a gender-sensitive approach by deliberately including women and women-led organizations among the interviewees.\

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 Following data collection, the interview recordings were systematically transcribed and analyzed to identify core views and recurring themes across different stakeholders and regions.\

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 The final analytical phase involved triangulating the interview findings with data gathered through desktop research and literature review, ensuring validation and enrichment of insights through multiple sources. The triangulation process strengthened the reliability of the study's conclusions and recommendations, providing a solid foundation for the development of actionable tools included within the SEED Expansion Box in order to support BDS providers and SEED companies.\page \pard\pardeftab720\tightenfactor0
\cf2 \par
\pard\tx1275\tx1533\pardeftab720\li1533\fi-1533\sa90\partightenfactor0
\ls12\ilvl0
\f3\b \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	2.	}
\f0 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Analysis of Trends and Opportunities in Green Industries\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1289\sa96\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 Africa\'92s green economy is gaining momentum as global climate urgency, local innovation, and shifting policy priorities converge. But for green businesses and entrepreneurial support organizations (BDS providers ) seeking to scale impactful solutions, the question is no longer why green industries matter \'97 it\'92s where the real opportunities lie, which sub-sectors are poised for regional expansion, and how to navigate the journey across borders.\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 This chapter provides a practical lens on the green industries landscape across Africa and globally. Rather than focusing narrowly on technology-driven ventures alone, we adopt a broader and more inclusive definition of green industries, encompassing both low-tech and high-tech, digital and physical, urban and rural solutions that contribute to environmental sustainability and climate resilience.\

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\fi1275\sa47\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 From \'93Green-Tech\'94 to Green Industries: A Broader, More Actionable Lens\par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1289\sa95\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 The terms green-tech, clean-tech, and climate-tech are often used interchangeably in policy circles and green businesses ecosystems. However, they tend to emphasize high-tech, science-based solutions, from smart grids and carbon-capture to AI-enabled agri-platforms. While these are important, they do not represent the full spectrum of the green industry.\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 In reality, green industries include a much wider array of business models, from regenerative farming and sustainable transport to waste-to-energy systems and bio-based packaging. Many of these are not software-first businesses, but rather scalable models embedded in local ecosystems, leveraging innovation in product design, value chain integration, community engagement, and resource management.\

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 For this reason, we use the term green industries throughout the report, aligning with the focus on inclusive, opportunity-rich sectors that can absorb and uplift green businesses at different stages of digital maturity. This framing is also more relatable for BDS providers working with a variety of green entrepreneurs, whether they\'92re launching an eco-tourism project in Benin, a plastic recycling business in Togo, or a solar irrigation service in Kenya.\

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\fi1275\sa155\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 Why This Matters for Expansion\page 
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1294\sa95\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 Understanding green industry trends is not only about identifying what\'92s growing, it\'92s about matching a green businesses' solution to the right markets, enablers, and partners. For BDS providers, this means refining support programs to prepare founders for regulatory navigation, market intelligence gathering, and capital mobilization in target countries.\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 This chapter, and the cross-country insights that follow, will serve as a market mapping tool. Thus, this chapter lays the groundwork for data-backed, opportunity-driven decisions about regional expansion.\


\pard\tx1275\tx1751\pardeftab720\li1275\fi-799\ri1289\sa241\partightenfactor0
\ls13\ilvl1
\f3\b \cf4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	2.1.	}
\f0 \cf4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Global and International Best Practices: Overview of Global Trends and Policies Shaping Green Industries and Scalable Business Models\cf2 \par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1289\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 Between 2021 and 2025, global momentum around green industries has moved from pilot projects to mainstream adoption. This shift presents concrete lessons and models that African green entrepreneurs and support organizations can learn from when planning regional or international growth.\par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 Key Global Trends Driving Opportunity\

\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1288\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls14\ilvl0
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f0\b \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Clean Energy is Scaling Fast
\f1\b0: Renewable energy is no longer a niche. By 2024, global installed capacity reached 4,448 GW, almost half of all energy generation worldwide, driven largely by solar and wind (World Economic Forum, 2024). For green businesses, this means growing downstream markets for solar services, clean energy access, and microgrid innovations.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1294\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls14\ilvl0
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f0\b \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Electric Mobility is Going Mainstream
\f1\b0: One in five new cars sold globally is electric (IEA, 2023). This creates opportunities across the value chain, from EV parts, battery recycling, and green logistics, to clean transport platforms.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1292\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls14\ilvl0
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f0\b \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Circular Economy and Waste Innovation Rising
\f1\b0: Global demand for sustainable packaging, upcycling, and zero-waste supply chains is creating export-ready models. African SMEs with circular models can leverage this momentum to tap into global buyers and investors.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1294\sa159\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls14\ilvl0
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f0\b \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Agri-Tech and Regenerative Farming Are Gaining Global Backing
\f1\b0: Climate-smart farming, vertical agriculture, and bio-based fertilizers are attracting venture capital and donor funding, especially in food-insecure regions. African agripreneurs can plug into this trend through digital traceability, processing innovations, and regionally aligned exports.
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 Green businesses and Ecosystem Insights\

\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1293\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls15\ilvl0
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f0\b \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Green businesses Are Attracting Capi
\fs22 tal
\f1\b0\fs24: In 2022, 25% of global VC funding went to climate tech (PwC, 2023), with major rounds for solutions in energy, carbon capture, and agriculture. Platforms like Climeworks and SunCulture show that tech-enabled models tied to clear environmental impact are now VC-friendly.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1288\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls15\ilvl0
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f0\b \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Policy Tailwinds Enable Growth
\f1\b0: From the EU\'92s Green Deal and the U.S. In\uc0\u64258 ation Reduction Act to China\'92s carbon neutrality targets, green-friendly policies have catalyzed green businesses growth through subsidies, procurement incentives, and regulatory clarity. African governments can emulate this by aligning national policies with green businesses needs.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1292\sa228\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls15\ilvl0
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f0\b \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Digital Tools Enable Scalability
\f1\b0: Globally, green businesses are leveraging digital tools like mobile finance, IoT sensors, blockchain traceability, and AI-driven analytics to scale faster. These tools reduce friction when expanding into new markets, and offer BDS providers proven templates to support green business acceleration.\par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 The global green transition is not just about climate, it\'92s about competitive positioning. African green businesses looking to scale can adopt and adapt tested models from other regions, while also positioning themselves as solution providers to global challenges. For BDS providers, understanding these trends is critical to helping founders design regionally scalable products, build investor-ready narratives, and align with policy momentum.\
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 The following sections will dive deeper into the green sectors that have gained global traction and explore which offer the best springboards for regional expansion from Africa.\


\pard\tx1275\tx1757\pardeftab720\li1757\fi-1757\sa11\partightenfactor0
\ls16\ilvl2
\f6\i \cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone 	2.1.1.\cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Overview of Global Green Industries Trends\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1296\sa236\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\i0 \cf2 This study identifies four major global trends shaping green industries between 2021 and 2025. The trends are ranked from most to least prominent, based on investment levels, policy support, adoption scale, and relevance for scalable green business models.\par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\fi1275\sa155\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 Global Trend #1 \'96 Renewable Energy Expansion\page 
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1289\sa5\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 Renewable energy is no longer a future vision, it\'92s today\'92s fastest-growing energy source and a cornerstone of global green economy growth. For green businesses, SMEs, and BDS providers in Africa, the rapid expansion of solar, wind, and other clean energy solutions offers clear signals: this is a sector with long-term demand, falling technology costs, and increasing policy support. Here are a few learnings :\par

\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1292\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls17\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f0\b \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Rapid Global Growth of Renewable Energy 
\f1\b0: Renewable energy has become the leading source of new power worldwide, with solar and wind driving record installations between 2021 and 2024. Over 90% of new global capacity in 2024 came from renewables, showing a clear shift away from fossil fuels (IRENA, 2025 ; World Economic Forum, 2024).\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1297\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls17\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f0\b \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Falling Costs and Economic Competitiveness 
\f1\b0: Plummeting technology costs have made solar and wind the cheapest energy sources in many markets. Solar PV prices dropped 12% in 2023, confirming their commercial viability and attracting large-scale investment.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1290\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls17\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f0\b \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Rising Opportunities for Africa and Green SMEs 
\f1\b0: Markets for solar mini-grids, PAYGO energy, and energy e
ciency are growing fast. Local SMEs that combine affordability, reliability, and digital tools are well-placed to lead, while cross-border solar trade strengthens regional green economies.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1294\sa153\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls17\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f0\b \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Innovative Business and Financing Models (EaaS and PAYGO) 
\f1\b0: The Energy-as-a-Service approach removes upfront costs by offering solar through long-term contracts, while green loans and crowdfunding attract investors. In Africa, PAYGO systems using mobile payments bring affordable, off-grid power to millions, driving inclusive clean energy growth.\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\fi1275\sa58\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 Global Trend #2 \'96 Electrification of Transport and Green Mobility\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa155\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 Electric mobility has shifted from a niche innovation to a global industrial revolution. Between 2019 and 2023, annual electric car sales grew from 2 million to nearly 14 million, with EVs accounting for 18% of new car sales worldwide by 2023 (IEA, 2023). For green businesses and SMEs in Africa, this transformation represents more than a shift in vehicles, it signals emerging opportunities across an entire green mobility value chain: manufacturing, logistics, servicing, financing, and infrastructure.\page 
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1293\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls18\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f0\b \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Industrial and Policy Drivers
\f1\b0: The EV boom is driven by strategic policy and industrial investment. China leads with nearly 60% of global EV sales in 2023 through subsidies and manufacturing mandates, while Europe reached 25% of new car sales and Norway over 80% (IEA, 2023). These policies deliver climate, economic, and competitiveness gains.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1290\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls18\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f0\b \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Africa\'92s Emerging Opportunities
\f1\b0: Africa\'92s EV market is nascent but promising. Growth areas include two - and three-wheeler electrification (e.g., Ecobodaa, Ampersand), battery-swapping, EV leasing, and solar charging. Supportive policies in Kenya, Rwanda, South Africa, and Benin (e.g. tax/VAT/custom exemptions for EV ) create openings for SMEs to localize clean mobility solutions.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1289\sa224\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls18\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f0\b \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone New Mobility Models
\f1\b0: New models like Mobility-as-a-Service and Battery-as-a-Service are reshaping transport. Companies run electric ride-hailing \uc0\u64258 eets, deploy charging networks, and lease swappable batteries. E-scooter and e-bike sharing in Europe and the U.S. show how electrification and digital platforms enable scalable, zero-emission mobility.\par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\fi1275\sa59\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 Global Trend #3 \'96 Circular Economy and Waste Innovation\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1292\sa237\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 The global shift from a \'93take-make-dispose\'94 model to a circular economy is creating significant new opportunities for entrepreneurs. Since 2021, governments, corporates, and investors have increasingly recognized waste as a resource, opening doors for green businesses to build businesses around recycling, reuse, and sustainable product redesign.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1289\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls19\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f0\b \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Policy Driving Circular Innovation
\f1\b0: Regulation is accelerating circular adoption. The EU\'92s 
\f6\i Circular Economy Action Plan 
\f1\i0 (2020 ) targets design, packaging, and extended producer responsibility. In Africa, Kenya, Rwanda, Morocco, and Senegal have plastic bag bans (even if the law is not yet the subject of a decree in Senegal); South Africa enforces producer responsibility; and Nigeria, Ghana, and Ivory Coast are introducing eco-taxes. These policies create predictable demand for SMEs offering local recycling and sustainable solutions.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1292\sa159\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls19\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f0\b \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Circular Business Opportunities in Africa
\f1\b0: Green businesses in Africa are thriving with e-waste recycling, upcycling agricultural or textile waste, composting, and product-as-a-service models. Informal waste systems dominate many cities, creating space for hybrid models that combine
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 collection, traceability, and value-added recycling. SMEs can start small and scale by partnering with municipalities or industries.\
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1289\sa235\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls20\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f0\b \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Circular Product and Recycling Innovations
\f1\b0: Entrepreneurs are turning waste into resources with product leasing, take-back, and specialized recycling. For example, Redwood Materials (U.S.) and Li-Cycle (Canada ) recycle EV batteries for lithium, cobalt, and nickel. Other ventures upcycle industrial food or textile waste into biochemicals, packaging, or feed, showing how circular innovation drives both profit and sustainability.\par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 For BDS providers, circular economy ventures often need support, from material sourcing and prototyping to certification, policy interpretation, and market education. With rising pressure on urban systems and natural resources, circular models are no longer optional, they\'92re key to sustainable economic growth and scalable impact.\
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\fi1275\sa58\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 Global Trend #4 \'96 Sustainable Agriculture and AgriTech\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1294\sa237\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 Agriculture is rapidly adopting sustainable practices as climate risks threaten food systems. Since 2019, innovation has shifted toward climate-smart, resource-e
cient models, including precision agriculture, controlled-environment farming, plant-based and cultivated proteins, and digital food waste solutions. Platforms like CropX and Taranis demonstrate scalability in both developed and emerging markets.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1297\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls21\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f0\b \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Capital and Policy Fueling Growth
\f1\b0: Global investors are funding climate-resilient biotech, robotics, and regenerative farming tools, while governments and multilaterals back climate-smart agriculture with subsidies and technical support. These \uc0\u64258 ows create opportunities for green entrepreneurs to align with public funding and scale through partnerships.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1289\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls21\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f0\b \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Opportunities for African Entrepreneurs
\f1\b0: In Africa, agriculture remains the largest employment sector. High-potential models include digital advisory services for smallholders, post-harvest storage and cold chains, climate-resilient crop processing (e.g., moringa, sorghum), organic inputs, and farm-to-market traceability platforms. Businesses that integrate smallholders, align with policy goals, and digitize logistics can scale faster.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1296\sa159\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls21\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f0\b \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone AgriTech and FoodTech Business Models
\f1\b0: Green businesses are innovating through mobile agronomic advice, microloans, pay-per-use equipment, farm-to-fork services, and alternative protein production. Models like
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 vertical integration of shade-grown coffee or plant-based foods combine sustainability with premium markets, attracting investment while reducing emissions and resource use. Examples in Africa include soy-based products in Benin reaching European markets.\


\pard\tx1275\tx1830\pardeftab720\li1830\fi-1830\sa11\partightenfactor0
\ls22\ilvl2
\f6\i \cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone 	2.1.2.\cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Global Policy Drivers of Green Industry Growth\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1290\sa5\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\i0 \cf2 Public policy has been a critical driver in shaping the global green economy. Since the Paris Agreement, leading governments have introduced ambitious frameworks to reduce emissions, incentivize clean technologies, and stimulate private-sector innovation. Below, we synthesize how four major global regions have activated green industry ecosystems and what practical lessons African BDS providers and green businesses can draw to support regional expansion and scale.\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\fi1275\sa59\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 European Union: Building the World\'92s Largest Green Market\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1302\sa237\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 The EU has strong rules, funding programs, and industrial plans for climate-friendly products and energy. Practical lessons:\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1292\partightenfactor0
\ls23\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone African green businesses can export or partner in sectors like renewable energy, transport, and recycling.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\tx2684\tx3971\tx4605\tx5453\tx6914\tx7423\tx7956\tx9502\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1290\sa239\partightenfactor0
\ls23\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone BDS providers can guide businesses on EU compliance, funding applications, and access to innovation programs.\par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\fi1275\sa59\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 United States \'96 Incentives for Green Tech\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1302\sa236\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 The U.S. has offered huge subsidies, tax credits, and support for clean tech R&D and EVs. Practical lessons:\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1300\partightenfactor0
\ls24\ilvl3
