\pard\tx1275\tx1728\pardeftab720\li1728\fi-1728\sa11\partightenfactor0
\ls52\ilvl1
\f3 \cf4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	2.	}
\f0 \cf4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone National and Regional Policy Enablers\cf2 \par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1290\sa5\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 While Africa\'92s green economy vision is broadly aligned through continental frameworks like Agenda 2063 and the African Continental Free Trade Area (AfCFTA), policy implementation varies significantly by country. This section distills the key enabling policies from the five target countries Kenya, South Africa, Senegal, Benin, and Togo highlighting how they shape green businesses ecosystems and regional expansion pathways in green industries.\par

\pard\tx1275\tx1828\pardeftab720\li1828\fi-1828\sa59\partightenfactor0
\ls53\ilvl2
\f6\i \cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	2.1.	}\cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Kenya: East Africa\'92s Green Policy Pioneer\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1289\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\i0 \cf2 Kenya has established itself as a leader in climate action and green innovation through robust national policies. The Climate Change Act (2016 ) institutionalized green planning at both national and county levels, enabling community-level implementation through the Financing Locally-Led Climate Action (FLLoCA ) program. Kenya\'92s Green Economy Strategy (2016\'962030 ) and Energy Act (2019 ) support renewables, energy e
ciency, and market-based mechanisms like net metering and competitive auctions for energy procurement.\par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 Green businesses and BDS providers benefit from VAT exemptions on clean energy imports, reduced corporate tax for EV manufacturers, and strong support\page \pard\pardeftab720\tightenfactor0
\cf2 \par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 institutions such as the Kenya Climate Innovation Center (KCIC). Special Economic Zones (SEZs ) offer additional fiscal incentives that lower the cost of clean-tech entrepreneurship. Kenya\'92s regional integration through the East African Community (EAC ) allows green businesses to scale into neighboring markets with relatively fewer barriers, especially in sectors like off-grid solar, waste-to-energy, and sustainable agriculture.\

\pard\tx1275\tx1900\pardeftab720\li1900\fi-1900\sa59\partightenfactor0
\ls54\ilvl2
\f6\i \cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	2.2.	}\cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone South Africa: Advanced Market with Institutional Complexity\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1290\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\i0 \cf2 South Africa\'92s policy infrastructure is among the most developed on the continent. The Renewable Energy IPP Procurement Programme (REIPPPP ) has attracted billions in private investment, creating a replicable model for clean energy scale-up. Other enabling instruments include Africa\'92s first Carbon Tax (2019), generous depreciation incentives for renewable equipment, and competitive tax breaks for energy storage and EV projects.\par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 However, implementation remains a challenge. Grid bottlenecks, the dominance of the public utility (Eskom), and policy reversals have at times undermined investor confidence. Still, entities like the Green Fund (DBSA ) and the Development Bank of Southern Africa (DBSA ) offer concessional finance and seed grants to green ventures. For BDS providers, South Africa offers a mature ecosystem for incubation and funding, while green businesses can benefit from AfCFTA-aligned trade to expand into neighboring countries like Namibia or Botswana. \'93South Africa doesn\'92t just have policy gaps, it has severe institutional misalignment that makes the green economy hard to navigate,\'94 Elize Hattingh from TIPS emphasized. Nick Allen further states that a lot of \'93climate-based regulations are often crafted to protect incumbents, creating barriers for new entrants\'94. These include outdated compliance frameworks or exclusionary certification processes. He advocated for forward-looking policies that enable innovation and allow green businesses to demonstrate viability despite initially not conforming to rigid rules.\

\pard\tx1275\tx1898\pardeftab720\li1898\fi-1898\sa59\partightenfactor0
\ls55\ilvl2
\f6\i \cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	2.3.	}\cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Senegal: Strategic Policies for Green green businesses Growth\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1290\sa159\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\i0 \cf2 Senegal\'92s framework is built around the Renewable Energy Law No. 2010-21 and strong national programs like Plan S\'e9n\'e9gal \'c9mergent Vert (PSE Vert). The government has combined clear policy signals with fiscal tools such as VAT and customs duty exemptions for solar, biogas, and energy-e
cient equipment.
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 Senegal was also an early adopter of Scaling Solar, offering competitive tenders for solar projects.\
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 What distinguishes Senegal is its direct green businesses support. Agencies like ADEPME and DER/FJ (Delegation for Rapid Entrepreneurship ) provide blended finance, grants, and incubation to youth- and women-led green businesses. The country\'92s participation in ECOWAS enables regional trade, and initiatives like the West African Clean Energy Corridor allow surplus renewable energy to be exported. These features make Senegal a viable launchpad for scaling sustainable energy, circular economy, and agri-tech innovations across West Africa.\

\pard\tx1275\tx1901\pardeftab720\li1901\fi-1901\sa58\partightenfactor0
\ls56\ilvl2
\f6\i \cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	2.4.	}\cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Benin: Gradual Progress and Regional Harmonization\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1289\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\i0 \cf2 Benin\'92s updated Electricity Code (2020 ) and Investment Code are opening its energy sector to private renewable players. Targeted incentives\'97such as VAT exemptions and the Off-Grid Clean Energy Facility (OCEF)\'97have attracted solar and mini-grid developers. Through the Millennium Challenge Corporation (MCC ) partnership, Benin has formalized its off-grid framework and catalyzed clean energy projects serving over 185,000 people.\par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 Benin\'92s alignment with UEMOA and ECOWAS ensures common trade and technical standards, allowing green businesses to scale within the region. Tax exemptions and streamlined permitting ease operational burdens, but gaps remain in capacity building and financing. For BDS providers, Benin offers a fertile ground for supporting rural energy access green businesses, solar assembly ventures, and green innovation focused on agriculture and bioenergy.\

\pard\tx1275\tx1909\pardeftab720\li1909\fi-1909\sa58\partightenfactor0
\ls57\ilvl2
\f6\i \cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	2.5.	}\cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Togo: Bold Reforms and Demand-Driven Incentives\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1293\sa240\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\i0 \cf2 Togo stands out for its pragmatic and inclusive approach. The Renewable Energy Law (2018 ) and the updated Electricity Code allow IPPs, net metering, and carbon market regulations. Programs like Projet CIZO combine subsidy-based solar distribution (through mobile payments ) with tax exemptions, making it easier for green businesses like Bboxx and SunCulture to enter and thrive.\par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 Additionally, Togo\'92s Investment Code links fiscal incentives to local job creation, and institutions like AT2ER (Rural Electrification Agency ) implement targeted clean energy programs. As a member of ECOWAS and the West African Power Pool, Togo is actively building the infrastructure for cross-border trade and\page \pard\pardeftab720\tightenfactor0
\cf2 \par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 certification alignment. This creates a favorable regulatory runway for green businesses scaling off-grid energy, e-mobility, and sustainable agriculture across Francophone West Africa.\

\pard\tx1275\tx1907\pardeftab720\li1907\fi-1907\sa59\partightenfactor0
\ls58\ilvl2
\f6\i \cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	2.6.	}\cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Regional Enablers for green businesses to scale\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1301\sa236\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\i0 \cf2 All five countries benefit from participation in broader continental and regional frameworks:\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\sa91\partightenfactor0
\ls59\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone AfCFTA simplifies trade and investment for green businesses across Africa.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1303\partightenfactor0
\ls59\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone ECOWAS (West Africa ) and EAC (East Africa ) enable mutual standards and electricity trade.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\tx2826\tx4156\tx5119\tx6336\tx7985\tx8888\tx9924\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1301\sa243\partightenfactor0
\ls59\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone SADC	(Southern Africa)	supports cross-border power pooling and certification.\par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 Continental efforts like the African Green Stimulus Programme (AGSP ) and Agenda 2063 reinforce national policies and finance mechanisms. The African Development Bank plays a key role by backing national climate strategies, green bonds, and blended finance facilities.\

\pard\tx1275\tx1866\pardeftab720\li1866\fi-1866\sa55\partightenfactor0
\ls60\ilvl2
\f6\i \cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	2.7.	}\cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Key Takeaway for green businesses and BDS providers\par

\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1294\partightenfactor0
\ls61\ilvl3
\f5\i0 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Kenya and South Africa offer the most mature regulatory and funding landscapes, ideal for scale-ready green businesses.\par
\ls61\ilvl3
\f5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Senegal and Togo provide high-potential, innovation-friendly markets for piloting models with strong public-private support.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\tx2837\tx3228\tx4366\tx5056\tx6364\tx6776\tx7826\tx8808\tx9433\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1298\sa243\partightenfactor0
\ls61\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Benin is evolving fast, especially in off-grid energy and regional collaboration.\par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 Policy alignment is improving, but BDS providers should prioritize technical assistance, policy dialogue, and knowledge sharing to help green businesses navigate evolving regulations and unlock regional market access.\


\pard\tx1276\tx1726\pardeftab720\li1726\fi-1726\sa11\partightenfactor0
\ls62\ilvl1
\f3\b \cf4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	3.	}
\f0 \cf4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Gaps and Misalignments for Cross-Border Expansion\cf2 \par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1288\sa159\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 Scaling green businesses in Africa requires navigating not just market demand but also complex regulatory and policy terrains. While intra-African trade is gaining momentum under initiatives like AfCFTA, green businesses and BDS providers still face a patchwork of national laws, standards, and institutional\page 
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 weaknesses. These structural misalignments hinder regional integration, in\uc0\u64258 ate green businesses costs, and reduce the effectiveness of BDS provider programs.\

\pard\tx1275\tx1826\pardeftab720\li1826\fi-1826\sa58\partightenfactor0
\ls63\ilvl2
\f6\i \cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	3.1.	}\cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Fragmented Regulations and Duplicated Compliance Costs\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1289\sa240\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\i0 \cf2 Green businesses expanding beyond their home country often face entirely new regulatory regimes. Business registration, licensing, IP protection, and tax codes differ by jurisdiction. For example, a solar company in Kenya must adjust its model when entering Tanzania due to differing energy licensing, tariffs, and consumer finance laws. Similarly, the OHADA civil law system in Francophone West Africa contrasts with the common law systems in Anglophone countries, increasing legal complexity and cost for expansion.\par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 Impact:\

\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\sa91\partightenfactor0
\ls64\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Green businesses must hire separate legal teams for each country.\par
\ls64\ilvl3
\f5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone It generates extra costs for the green businesses\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1298\partightenfactor0
\ls64\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone BDS providers cannot offer one-size-fits-all acceleration or mentoring programs.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1297\sa243\partightenfactor0
\ls64\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Investors face high due diligence costs and inconsistent enforceability of contracts.\par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 In South Africa, Elize Hattingh, one of the interviewees of the study from TIPS, critiques the siloed structure of government departments (DSBD, DTIC, DFFE), where each has its own green economy program without coordination. \'93Licensing delays, particularly in the waste and water sectors, also came up where entrepreneurs face an 18-month waiting time with no transparency. \cf9 Some departments don\'92t even know who\'92s responsible internally. A client once spent a year chasing a single waste license.\'94\cf2 \

\pard\tx1275\tx1898\pardeftab720\li1898\fi-1898\sa99\partightenfactor0
\ls65\ilvl2
\f6\i \cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	3.2.	}\cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Trade Barriers and Non-Harmonized Standards\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1289\sa240\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\i0 \cf2 Many green products (e.g., solar panels, clean cookstoves, EVs ) are subject to varied import duties and certification requirements. Even within regional blocs like ECOWAS or EAC, tariffs and technical standards differ. For example, Kenya incentivizes electric vehicle adoption with reduced VAT, but a regional tariff sets EV import duties at 35%. Neighboring Ethiopia, meanwhile, removed all EV duties.\par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 Impact:\page \pard\pardeftab720\tightenfactor0
\cf2 \par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1294\partightenfactor0
\ls66\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Hardware green businesses face high entry costs and must duplicate product certifications.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\tx2729\tx4061\tx5552\tx6991\tx8921\tx9575\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1295\partightenfactor0
\ls66\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone BDS providers supporting clean-tech manufacturing see limited cross-border scalability.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\sa99\partightenfactor0
\ls66\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Regional trade is deterred, despite AfCFTA promises.\par

\pard\tx1275\tx1896\pardeftab720\li1896\fi-1896\sa99\partightenfactor0
\ls67\ilvl2
\f6\i \cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	3.3.	}\cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Lack of Mutual Recognition of Certifications\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1294\sa240\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\i0 \cf2 Green businesses often need third-party certifications (e.g., ISO standards, organic/agro-ecological labels, solar product quality), but these are rarely recognized across borders. This leads to costly recertification processes when entering new markets.\par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 Impact:\

\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\sa91\partightenfactor0
\ls68\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Green businesses cannot scale e
ciently\par
\ls68\ilvl3
\f5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Green product quality assurance remains fragmented.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\sa99\partightenfactor0
\ls68\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone BDS providers cannot streamline compliance support across borders.\par

\pard\tx1275\tx1899\tx1899\pardeftab720\li1899\fi-1899\sa99\partightenfactor0
\ls69\ilvl2
\f6\i \cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	3.4.	}\cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Limited Access to Cross-Border Green Finance\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1290\sa240\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\i0 \cf2 Capital remains concentrated in a few markets like South Africa or Kenya. Interest rates in many African countries range between 13% and 30%, making debt unaffordable. Most African countries are sub-investment grade, discouraging private and institutional investors. Currency risks, capital controls, and lack of regional credit frameworks exacerbate the financing gap.\par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 Impact:\

\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\sa91\partightenfactor0
\ls70\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Green businesses face di
culty securing capital for regional expansion.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\sa90\partightenfactor0
\ls70\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Investors demand higher returns or avoid smaller markets altogether.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\tx2789\tx4166\tx5078\tx6578\tx7639\tx9402\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1289\sa8\partightenfactor0
\ls70\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone BDS providers must constantly broker relationships between country-specific financiers and green businesses.\par

\pard\tx1275\tx1907\pardeftab720\li1907\fi-1907\sa98\partightenfactor0
\ls71\ilvl2
\f6\i \cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	3.5.	}\cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Infrastructure and Connectivity Constraints\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa159\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\i0 \cf2 Energy, logistics, and digital infrastructure deficits constrain cross-border green business growth. Incomplete power pool linkages, poor border facilities, and limited EV charging corridors fragment regional green markets.\page 
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 Examples:\

\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1292\partightenfactor0
\ls72\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Solar green businesses struggle to serve border communities without coordinated grid infrastructure.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1300\partightenfactor0
\ls72\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Clean mobility companies must invest in separate charging networks in each country.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1299\sa7\partightenfactor0
\ls72\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Cold chain and logistics gaps restrict agro-processing and perishable exports.\par

\pard\tx1275\tx1906\tx1906\pardeftab720\li1906\fi-1906\sa98\partightenfactor0
\ls73\ilvl2
\f6\i \cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	3.6.	}\cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Policy Unpredictability and Inconsistent Incentives\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1298\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\i0 \cf2 In many countries, green incentives are introduced and withdrawn with little notice. Green businesses benefiting from tax breaks in one year may face new VAT requirements the next. This instability makes long-term planning risky and discourages expansion.\par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 Examples:\
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\sa91\partightenfactor0
\ls74\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Kenya introduced, then reversed, EV tax breaks within two years.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\tx3424\tx4421\tx5538\tx5964\tx6991\tx8251\tx9026\tx10108\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1290\sa244\partightenfactor0
\ls74\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Renewable energy projects in several countries were delayed or renegotiated due to unclear or shifting feed-in tariff policies.\par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 Impact:\

\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\sa91\partightenfactor0
\ls75\ilvl3
