\fs22 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone All (general business climate)
\fs24 \cf0 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone 
\f0\b\fs20 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone \cell \lastrow\row
\pard\pardeftab720\sa1\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf2 \par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa96\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 The green industry in Africa holds immense promise for driving sustainable development, from expanding energy access with renewables to creating jobs in e-mobility and circular economy ventures. Yet, as this analysis has detailed, the continent\'92s patchwork of regulatory and policy environments poses significant hurdles to realizing that promise at a regional scale. Fragmented regulations, inconsistent standards, isolated certification systems, financial and infrastructural bottlenecks, erratic policies, uneven ESG enforcement, and divergent legal frameworks all conspire to keep green entrepreneurs and investors operating in silos. These gaps hinder not only individual business growth but also the integration of African markets, which is necessary to achieve cost e
ciencies and innovation spillovers in the green economy.\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 For Africa to unlock the full potential of its green industries, greater regional coordination and policy harmonization is imperative. Streamlining and aligning regulations, from technical standards to investment rules \'96 will reduce the burden on green businesses and give confidence to investors that Africa can be approached as a cohesive opportunity, not just 54 separate ones. Investors and development partners can also play a role by supporting regional guarantee instruments, multi-country financing facilities, and knowledge-sharing platforms that reduce the perceived risk of cross-border ventures. Ecosystem support organizations can advocate in unison for policy changes and help bridge information gaps between countries.\


\pard\tx1276\tx1541\pardeftab720\li1541\fi-1541\sa7\partightenfactor0
\ls84\ilvl0
\f3\b \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	4.	}
\f0 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Investment and Financing Landscape\par

\pard\tx1275\tx1669\pardeftab720\li1669\fi-1669\sa11\partightenfactor0
\ls85\ilvl1
\f3 \cf4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone 	4.1.
\f0 \cf4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Mapping Funding Mechanisms and Gaps\cf2 \par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1290\sa159\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 Green businesses in Africa face a fragmented and uneven funding landscape, characterized by geographic and sectoral disparities, a heavy reliance on donor-driven mechanisms, and a significant early-stage capital gap. While there is\page 
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 growing investor interest in climate-tech, the \uc0\u64258 ow of capital to SEED-stage ventures and green SMEs remains shallow compared to the scale of Africa\'92s green transition needs. This section unpacks the core funding channels, identifies gaps facing green businesses, and highlights lessons from five African countries to guide BDS providers and seed-stage innovators on how to tap into climate finance.\

\pard\tx1275\tx1788\tx1788\pardeftab720\li1275\fi-762\ri1297\sa8\partightenfactor0
\ls86\ilvl2
\f6\i \cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone 	4.1.1.\cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Overview of Green Funding Instruments and Key Sources of Green Finance in Africa\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1295\sa236\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\i0 \cf2 Green Funding Instruments: Green businesses can access funding from several categories of capital providers:\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1289\partightenfactor0
\ls87\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f0\b \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Development Finance Institutions (DFIs)
\f1\b0: AfDB, IFC, Proparco, KfW, FMO, etc., channel funds into climate projects and blended finance structures.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1293\partightenfactor0
\ls87\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f0\b \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Climate Finance Facilities
\f1\b0: Green Climate Fund (GCF), Global Environment Facility (GEF), Climate Investment Funds (CIF).\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1290\partightenfactor0
\ls87\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f0\b \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Impact Investors & VCs
\f1\b0: Novastar, Catalyst Fund, Satgana, E3 Capital, Acumen, etc.\par
\ls87\ilvl3
\f5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f0\b \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Donor Agencies
\f1\b0: GIZ, Enabel, AFD, Norad, SEforAll \'96 often fund incubators or pilot projects.\par
\ls87\ilvl3
\f5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f0\b \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Foundations & Philanthropy
\f1\b0: IKEA Foundation, Shell Foundation, DOEN Foundation, among others.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1302\sa243\partightenfactor0
\ls87\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f0\b \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Sovereign Green Bonds
\f1\b0: Used in Kenya, Nigeria, Egypt, Benin, etc., to raise public funds for green infrastructure.\par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 Key Challenge: These instruments are often top-down, project-specific, and hard to access for small, early-stage ventures.\

\pard\tx1275\tx1830\pardeftab720\li1830\fi-1830\sa55\partightenfactor0
\ls88\ilvl2
\f6\i \cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone 	4.1.2.\cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Key Sources of Green Finance in Africa\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1995\sa59\partightenfactor0

\f0\i0\b \cf2 Public Funding (Domestic Government Budgets)\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\sa59\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 Governments have initiated climate-aligned national funds and policy tools:\par

\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1297\partightenfactor0
\ls89\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone South Africa created a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.dffe.gov.za/GreenFund"}}{\fldrslt \cf7 \ul \ulc7 Green Fund}}\cf7 \cf2 and introduced a Carbon Tax (with reinvested revenues).\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1292\sa159\partightenfactor0
\ls89\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Kenya implemented a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://new.kenyalaw.org/akn/ke/act/2016/11/eng@2023-09-15"}}{\fldrslt \cf7 \ul \ulc7 Climate Change Act}}\cf7 \cf2 with a National Climate Fund and climate budget coding.
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 However, limited fiscal space and debt constraints restrict public capacity to scale green investments. Governments primarily fund early-stage pilots, enabling infrastructure or offering subsidies and guarantees to catalyze private capital. Also, though Kenya has seen a rise in climate-focused VCs (e.g. E3 Capital, MyMassif), Nduwayezu warns that green businesses still face underfunding. \'93Opportunities often don\'92t attract commercial capital due to unclear returns and limited local R&D.\'94\
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 Private Sector Financing: While private finance grew to ~$8 billion (2019\'962021), it still represents only 18% of climate finance. Investment is concentrated in:\
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\sa91\partightenfactor0
\ls90\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Utility-scale renewable energy (e.g. solar farms via IPPs).\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\sa63\partightenfactor0
\ls90\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Off-grid solar innovations, especially PAYG models.\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 But adaptation sectors (agritech, water, waste management ) remain underfinanced due to lower return profiles and perceived risks. Local SMEs struggle to access credit due to high collateral demands and short loan tenors.\
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 Opportunity for green businesses: Sector focus matters. Energy green businesses benefit from mature PPP models, while climate-smart agriculture and waste innovators should look toward blended finance or philanthropic co-funding.\
\pard\pardeftab720\li1995\sa58\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 Multilateral and Bilateral Institutions\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1302\sa237\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 MDBs and climate funds (e.g., AfDB, World Bank, GCF ) account for over 43% of Africa\'92s climate finance, supporting:\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\sa91\partightenfactor0
\ls91\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Large infrastructure (solar, hydro, wind).\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\sa90\partightenfactor0
\ls91\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Adaptation projects (coastal resilience, drought-proof agriculture).\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\sa63\partightenfactor0
\ls91\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Policy and capacity-building initiatives.\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 Some innovative models:\

\pard\tx1635\tx1995\tx2923\tx4423\tx5417\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1298\partightenfactor0
\ls92\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone AfDB\'92s Sustainable Energy Fund for Africa (SEFA ) offers technical assistance and concessional loans.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\sa59\partightenfactor0
\ls92\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Green Climate Fund provides project grants and supports local institutions.\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1995\sa59\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 Philanthropic and Civil Society Capital\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1302\sa155\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 While smaller in volume, philanthropic finance is risk-tolerant and \uc0\u64258 exible, funding:\page 
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\sa91\partightenfactor0
\ls93\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Grassroots pilots\par
\ls93\ilvl3
\f5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Climate advocacy\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\sa59\partightenfactor0
\ls93\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Community adaptation\par

\pard\tx1275\tx1828\pardeftab720\li1828\fi-1828\sa59\partightenfactor0
\ls94\ilvl2
\f6\i \cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone 	4.1.3.\cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Structural Financing Gaps: What Holds green businesses back\par

\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1288\partightenfactor0
\ls95\ilvl0
\f2\i0 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	1.	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone \'93Missing Middle\'94 Capital: green businesses often fall between micro-grants and large infrastructure funds. Most green SMEs need $50K\'96$250K, but:\par
\pard\tx2355\tx2715\pardeftab720\li2715\fi-2715\sa91\partightenfactor0
\ls95\ilvl1
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9b	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Commercial banks avoid early-stage risk.\par
\ls95\ilvl1
\f5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9b	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Venture capital is nascent for cleantech/agritech.\par
\pard\tx2355\tx2715\pardeftab720\li2715\fi-2715\sa94\partightenfactor0
\ls95\ilvl1
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9b	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Public instruments rarely trickle to small firms.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1291\sa1\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls96\ilvl0
\f2 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	2.	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Collateral and Risk Perception: Local banks often demand land, buildings, or large cash \uc0\u64258 ows \'96 infeasible for lean green businesses.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1288\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls96\ilvl0
\f2 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	3.	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Geographic and Sector Bias: Most capital \uc0\u64258 ows to East and Southern Africa, and to mitigation sectors (energy). Waste, mobility, and water get less attention.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1300\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls96\ilvl0
\f2 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	4.	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Complex Application Processes: International funds have di
cult eligibility criteria, application processes, and long disbursement timelines \'96 especially hard for small teams to manage.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1302\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls96\ilvl0
\f2 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	5.	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Limited Financial Products: Few banks or DFIs offer financial instruments suited to green green businesses realities, such as:\par
\pard\tx2355\tx2715\pardeftab720\li2715\fi-2715\sa91\partightenfactor0
\ls97\ilvl1
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9b	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Revenue-based loans\par
\ls97\ilvl1
\f5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9b	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Convertible grants\par
\pard\tx2355\tx2715\pardeftab720\li2715\fi-2715\sa99\partightenfactor0
\ls97\ilvl1
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9b	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Impact-linked finance\par

\pard\tx1275\tx1831\tx1831\pardeftab720\li1831\fi-1831\sa98\partightenfactor0
\ls98\ilvl2
\f6\i \cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	4.	}\cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Blended Finance Structures: Successes and Limitations\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1296\sa236\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\i0 \cf2 Blended finance, combining public/donor funds with private capital, is increasingly used to de-risk green investments. Tools include:\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\sa91\partightenfactor0
\ls99\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone First-loss capital (e.g., {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://thegreenoutcomesfund.co.za/"}}{\fldrslt \cf7 \ul \ulc7 Green Outcomes Fund}}\cf7 \cf2 in South Africa).\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1301\partightenfactor0
\ls99\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Results-based finance (e.g., Energising Development (EnDev ) results-based funding for PAYG solar in Benin, Kenya).\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\sa63\partightenfactor0
\ls99\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Guarantees (e.g., AfDB Partial Risk Guarantees for renewable energy).\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 But blended finance tends to target infrastructure-scale projects, not green businesses. Only a few programs (e.g., AfricaGoGreen Fund, SEFA ) offer SME-friendly terms. Elize Hattingh from TIPS insists that blended finance models should combine microgrants, working capital, and deferred equity where possible.\page \pard\pardeftab720\tightenfactor0
\cf2 \par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1302\sa244\qj\partightenfactor0

\f6\i \cf9 \'93We need financial tools that match the reality of informal and micro-business models, not just commercial investors.\'94\cf2 \par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1295\sa4\qj\partightenfactor0

\f9\i0 \cf2 Hattingh\'92s team designed a Switch On Green fund model (now supported by the EU and local DFIs ) to provide \'8830,000 microfinance windows for underfunded green entrepreneurs.
\f1 \par

\pard\tx1275\tx1839\tx1839\pardeftab720\li1839\fi-1839\sa98\partightenfactor0
\ls100\ilvl2
\f6\i \cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	5.	}\cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Grant Ecosystem: Vital but Not Scalable\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa237\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\i0 \cf2 Many green entrepreneurs rely on competitive grants, especially in Francophone countries:\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\sa90\partightenfactor0
\ls101\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Togo and Benin: support from Enabel, UNDP, and MCC grants.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\sa91\partightenfactor0
\ls101\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Senegal: DER-FJ innovation fund (up to $15K per venture).\par
\ls101\ilvl3
\f5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Kenya: numerous donor-funded challenges (e.g., GSMA, KCIC).\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1292\sa244\partightenfactor0
\ls101\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone South Africa: WWF & Nedbank Green Fund, Green Climate Fund, Nedbank ReNew Accelerator.\par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 While useful for pilot funding, grants are non-recurring, fragmented, and administratively heavy. Few programs offer follow-on funding or clear graduation to equity/debt.\

\pard\tx1275\tx1837\tx1837\pardeftab720\li1837\fi-1837\sa99\partightenfactor0
\ls102\ilvl2
\f6\i \cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	6.	}\cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Growth-Stage Financing: Clustered in Select Hubs\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1292\sa237\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\i0 \cf2 There is more capital available for growth-stage ventures in Kenya, South Africa, and Senegal than in Togo or Benin:\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1289\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls103\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Kenya: Key destination for climate-tech capital in Africa ($1.1B+ raised by Kenyan climate green businesses since 2019).\par
\ls103\ilvl3
\f5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone South Africa: Larger domestic institutional investors (e.g., Futuregrowth\'92s ESG portfolios). Holocene is one of the few active early-stage investors writing $50K\'96$250K tickets for green businesses in Southern Africa. Most green businesses need a minimum of $250K to get traction, but grants and angel capital are severely lacking. Holocene combines capital with embedded support: \'93We typically invest $50K\'96$100K cash and match that with $50K\'96$100K of hands-on support to build the business until it\'92s ready for scale.\'94 says Josh Rominder (Holocone).\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\sa62\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls103\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Francophone West Africa: Few funds, heavily donor-dependent.\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 Key Challenges:\page \pard\pardeftab720\tightenfactor0
\cf2 \par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1295\partightenfactor0
\ls104\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Lack of revenue visibility in many models (e.g., pay-as-you-go, carbon credits).\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\sa91\partightenfactor0
\ls104\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Missing middle: Few funds write $500K\'96$2M checks.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\sa59\partightenfactor0
\ls104\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Currency risk: Hard-currency financing in volatile FX environments.\par

\pard\tx1275\tx1770\pardeftab720\li1770\fi-1770\sa99\partightenfactor0
\ls105\ilvl2
\f10\i \cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	7.	}
\f6 \cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Key Takeaways\cf2 \par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1288\sa236\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\i0 \cf2 Africa\'92s green finance ecosystem is growing, but SEED-stage green businesses remain underserved. While development banks and governments are investing billions into green infrastructure, most of that capital bypasses green businesses. For green entrepreneurs, this creates a gap, but also a powerful opportunity: to position your venture where impact, innovation, and investment converge.\par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1995\sa59\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 Understand the Funding Landscape\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1289\sa237\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 Green capital in Africa comes from multiple sources, but not all are green businesses-friendly:\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\sa90\partightenfactor0
\ls106\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Donors and climate funds prefer large, de-risked projects.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\sa91\partightenfactor0
\ls106\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Impact VCs exist but concentrate on proven models (e.g. PAYG solar).\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\sa63\partightenfactor0
\ls106\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Grants and prizes are accessible but highly competitive.\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 What to do:\

\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1290\partightenfactor0
\ls107\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Research and target funders aligned with your green businesses stage and sector.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1292\partightenfactor0
\ls107\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Start with catalytic funding (bootstrapping, grants, competitions ) before approaching VCs.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1302\sa239\partightenfactor0
\ls107\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Build partnerships with local NGOs or BDS providers to access blended finance.\par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1995\sa59\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 Pick the Right Sector Strategy\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\sa60\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 Some sectors receive more investor attention and ecosystem support:\par

\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1296\partightenfactor0
\ls108\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Solar and off-grid energy green businesses benefit from proven business models and existing funds.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1288\sa243\partightenfactor0
\ls108\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Waste, mobility, water, and sustainable agri-tech face capital gaps but offer differentiation and local impact.\par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 What to do:\page \pard\pardeftab720\tightenfactor0
\cf2 \par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1302\partightenfactor0
\ls109\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Position your venture in a sector with strong public or DFI support in your country (e.g. mobility in Kenya, circular economy in South Africa).\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1299\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls109\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Quantify your environmental and development impact (CO\uc0\u8322 saved, jobs created, etc.), this matters to climate investors.\par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1995\sa59\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 Be Finance-Ready\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\sa236\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 Many green businesses fail to raise money because they are not structured or documented well:\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1296\sa244\partightenfactor0
\ls110\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Weak governance, unclear revenue models, or missing financial forecasts scare investors off.\par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 What to do:\

\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\sa91\partightenfactor0
\ls111\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Get investor-ready: build your pitch deck, business plan, and impact logic.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1295\partightenfactor0
\ls111\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Track your metrics (customer acquisition cost, lifetime value, emissions avoided, etc.).\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\sa59\partightenfactor0
\ls111\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Clean up your legal structure and ensure compliance (IP, taxes, etc.).\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1995\sa59\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 Explore Collaborative Financing Models\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1288\sa236\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 Most green funds have high minimums ($250K+), too big for early-stage green businesses. That\'92s where creative models come in:\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\sa91\partightenfactor0
\ls112\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Pool resources with similar ventures\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1275\fi-555\ri4354\sa4\partightenfactor0
\ls112\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Join cooperatives or shared investment vehicles What to do:\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1297\partightenfactor0
\ls112\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Join green businesses communities and local hubs that connect you with shared funding mechanisms.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\tx2865\tx3321\tx4721\tx5173\tx6755\tx7418\tx8184\tx9153\tx10099\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1298\sa239\partightenfactor0
\ls112\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Apply to incubators or accelerators that offer pooled capital or introductions to institutional funders.\par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1995\sa59\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 Engage in the Ecosystem\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1288\sa236\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 Green businesses don\'92t scale in isolation. Engage with the wider policy and ecosystem landscape:\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\sa91\partightenfactor0
\ls113\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Join climate innovation networks\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\sa159\partightenfactor0
\ls113\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Build visibility through events, pilot programs, and partnerships
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 What to do:\

\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1294\partightenfactor0
\ls114\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Stay informed on government tenders, climate funds, and green public procurement opportunities.\par
\ls114\ilvl3
\f5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Collaborate with BDS providers and development agencies to pilot your solution and boost credibility.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1300\sa243\partightenfactor0
\ls114\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Contribute to shaping regulations (e.g. e-mobility laws, circular economy frameworks ) by engaging with policy forums or associations.\par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 In summary, the climate transition needs grassroots innovation. green businesses are at the forefront of solving energy access, waste, mobility, and agriculture challenges, but unlocking capital requires strategic positioning, financial readiness, and strong networks. With the right approach, you can scale impactful green solutions in Africa and beyond.\

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\sa99\partightenfactor0

\f6\i \cf5 4.1.8. Key Strategic Takeaways for green businesses\cf2 \par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1296\sa8\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\i0 \cf2 Based on the above analysis of the investments and capital in the green industry of Africa and the 5 represented countries, here are the key takeaway that green businesses can use:\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 Table: Table of Investment and Finance Opportunities\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa7\partightenfactor0

\fs5 \cf2 \par

