\f3\b \cf4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	2.	}
\f0 \cf4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Inclusive Investment Trends (Women, Youth)\cf2 \par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa5\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 As Africa moves toward a green and inclusive economic transformation, empowering women and youth has become essential for accelerating innovation, job creation, and resilient communities. Across the five countries studied, Kenya, South Africa, Senegal, Benin, and Togo, progressive policies and dedicated investment schemes are emerging, yet actual capital \uc0\u64258 ow remains limited and uneven. This section provides strategic insights for women and youth-led SEED-stage companies willing to expand in new markets to identify the most promising countries, sectors, and investment pathways.\par

\pard\tx1275\tx1830\pardeftab720\li1830\fi-1830\sa58\partightenfactor0
\ls116\ilvl2
\f6\i \cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	2.1.	}\cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Policy Momentum for Inclusion\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1299\sa237\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\i0 \cf2 Across all five countries, national climate strategies and development frameworks have incorporated gender and youth as key pillars:\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1289\partightenfactor0
\ls117\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Kenya integrates gender/youth in its 
\f6\i Vision 2030 
\f1\i0 and 
\f6\i Climate Change Act (2016)
\f1\i0 , mandating inclusive green initiatives.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\tx2918\tx3998\tx4702\tx6065\tx7583\tx8727\tx9619\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1300\partightenfactor0
\ls117\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone South Africa\'92s	
\f6\i Just Transition Framework	
\f1\i0 embeds social equity, emphasizing historically marginalized groups like Black women and youth.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1288\partightenfactor0
\ls117\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Senegal and Benin promote inclusive entrepreneurship and job creation via national development plans and youth employment strategies.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1294\sa242\partightenfactor0
\ls117\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Togo\'92s 
\f6\i National Strategy for Financial Inclusion (2021\'962025) 
\f1\i0 focuses on reducing financial exclusion among women and youth.\par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 Despite these high-level commitments, implementation and financing remain weak, many inclusive references have not been translated into scaled-up funding pipelines.\

\pard\tx1275\tx1903\pardeftab720\li1903\fi-1903\sa59\partightenfactor0
\ls118\ilvl2
\f6\i \cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	2.2.	}\cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Where the Money Goes: Gender and Youth-Focused Funds\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1290\sa240\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\i0 \cf2 Green businesses can access several funding vehicles, although many remain pilot-scale or donor-backed:\par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 Women Targeted Funds\

\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1290\sa155\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls119\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone South Africa leads with institutional players like the National Empowerment Fund and Women\'92s Development Business Investment Holdings, especially active in renewables, health, and manufacturing.
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1291\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls119\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Kenya is supported by impact investors like Acumen, One Acre Fund and GroFin, targeting agri-tech, fintech, and climate-smart innovations led by women.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1302\sa233\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls119\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone AfDB\'92s AFAWA program is targeting $5 billion for women entrepreneurs, with activity beginning in Togo, Senegal, and Benin, often via local partner banks.\par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 Youth Targeted Instruments\

\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1297\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls120\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Kenya\'92s YEDF and Ajira Digital Program offer grants, digital literacy, and e-commerce tools to youth-led ventures.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1288\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls120\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Senegal\'92s DER/FJ (D\'e9l\'e9gation G\'e9n\'e9rale \'e0 l\'92Entrepreneuriat Rapide ) has already financed 20,000+ youth and women-led business, offering soft loans and guarantees.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1294\sa2\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls120\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Togo\'92s FAIEJ and FNFI offer microfinance to youth, though uptake is often concentrated in urban centers.\par

\pard\tx1275\tx1901\pardeftab720\li1901\fi-1901\sa59\partightenfactor0
\ls121\ilvl2
\f6\i \cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	2.3.	}\cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Sectoral Hotspots for Inclusive Green Investment\par

