\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9b	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone M-Auto (Togo ) and Ampersand (Kenya ) offer potential role models, yet inclusive financing here is minimal.
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1301\sa3\qj\partightenfactor0

\f6\i \cf9 Green businesses Tip
\f1\i0 \cf9: Choose sectors where both impact and funding appetite intersect (and work on your storytelling/pitch). Agri-tech, micro solar distribution, and digital tools currently offer the highest overlap.\cf2 \par

\pard\tx1275\tx1903\pardeftab720\li1903\fi-1903\sa55\partightenfactor0
\ls129\ilvl2
\f6\i \cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	4.	}\cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Barriers Still Facing Inclusive Founders\par

\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1301\partightenfactor0
\ls130\ilvl3
\f5\i0 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Collateral requirements: Women/youth often lack formal assets, hindering bank loans.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1295\partightenfactor0
\ls130\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Information gaps: Many founders are unaware of available funds or lack the skills to apply.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1290\partightenfactor0
\ls130\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Bias in venture funding: Less than 10% of VC capital in Africa reaches female founders (Briter Bridges, 2023).\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1296\sa7\partightenfactor0
\ls130\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Lack of risk capital: In Benin and Togo, green businesses rely heavily on short-term donor programs without sustainable follow-on funding.\par

\pard\tx1275\tx1912\tx1912\pardeftab720\li1912\fi-1912\sa58\partightenfactor0
\ls131\ilvl2
\f6\i \cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	5.	}\cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Strategic Recommendations for Women and Youth Entrepreneurs\par

\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1288\sa1\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls132\ilvl0
\f2\i0 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	1.	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Leverage Donor-Linked National Programs: Access local arms of AfDB, DER/FJ, FAIEJ, and FNFI for blended finance and guarantees.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1289\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls132\ilvl0
\f2 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	2.	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Utilized DFI Supported Sectors to Gain Access to New Markets and Target Known Support Sectors: Research green sectors within your region and the regions of the other 5 SEED countries that are supported financially by large DFIs (World Bank, African Development Bank, AU, Southern African Development Bank ) projects and then engage the projects in what financial tools, programs and incentives they have that one can access and utilize. There are a lot of DFI supported projects in Africa that provide inclusion and financial initiatives for agriculture and gender participation, solar micro-grid implementation, and pay-as-you-go solar product and service markets and e-mobility integration and mobilization.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1288\sa1\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls132\ilvl0
\f2 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	3.	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Join Inclusive Investment Platforms: Participate in platforms like AFAWA, GroFin, or YEDF, which often bundle finance with mentorship.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1289\sa240\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls132\ilvl0
\f2 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	4.	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Use Impact Metrics: Frame your green green businesses\'92s impact using metrics that appeal to gender-lens and youth-lens investors (e.g., jobs created, communities served, CO\uc0\u8322 avoided).\par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 While inclusive green finance remains underdeveloped, public programs and gender-lens investment vehicles are expanding across the continent. Agriculture, energy access, and digital solutions remain the best entry points for women and youth-led SEED companies. To unlock these opportunities, founders must engage\page \pard\pardeftab720\tightenfactor0
\cf2 \par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 with national development plans, use impact narratives effectively, and explore donor-partnered financial tools. With growing political momentum and select regional success stories, the time is ripe for inclusive green businesses to lead Africa\'92s green transition.\


\pard\tx1275\tx1600\tx1600\pardeftab720\li1600\fi-1600\sa7\partightenfactor0
\ls133\ilvl0
\f3\b \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	5.	}
\f0 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Women and Youth in Green Industries\par

\pard\tx1276\tx1666\pardeftab720\li1666\fi-1666\sa11\partightenfactor0
\ls134\ilvl1
\f3 \cf4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone 	5.1.
\f0 \cf4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Sector Participation Mapping\cf2 \par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1288\sa240\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 Africa\'92s shift to a green economy offers a transformative opportunity to reshape how economic growth intersects with gender and generational equity. While women and youth are disproportionately vulnerable to the impacts of climate change, due to structural inequalities in access to land, finance, and education, they are also key drivers of inclusive innovation and labor force expansion in emerging green sectors.\par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 This section maps the participation of women and youth in key green sectors, specifically renewable energy, agriculture and agritech, and circular economy/manufacturing, across Kenya, South Africa, Senegal, Benin, and Togo. The objective is to understand where inclusive entrepreneurship is most active, where structural barriers persist, and how founders can position themselves for regional scale and sectoral alignment.\

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 Methodology\

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 Participation levels are categorized as High, Medium, or Low, based on:\

\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\sa91\partightenfactor0
\ls135\ilvl0
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
