\f1\i0\fs24 \cf2 Key Insights:\par

\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1289\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls141\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Youth-led green businesses are innovating in plastic-to-brick recycling, bio-composting, and fashion upcycling, particularly in Benin and South Africa.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1298\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls141\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Women\'92s engagement tends to be concentrated in collection and small-scale recycling; few make the leap to formal manufacturing or product design.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1292\sa227\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls141\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Informal waste systems dominate in most countries, but some efforts (e.g. #Waste2Cash in Benin ) are formalizing informal jobs and enabling green businesses creation.\par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 Expansion Takeaway: Circular economy entrepreneurs have room to replicate scalable, low capex models (e.g. plastic micro-factories or compost hubs). Exporting successful recycling models from South Africa or Kenya to West Africa\page \pard\pardeftab720\tightenfactor0
\cf2 \par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 could be driven through regional partnerships, especially if aligned with circular economy roadmaps (e.g. Benin, Senegal). Lisa Johansson from Greencape emphasizes that: \'93Women and youth are notably active in sustainable agriculture and some circular economy niches. However, barriers like access to feedstock and market entry persist, though these are not uniquely gendered. Enthusiasm around supporting female founders exists, but structural job creation for youth remains limited.\'94\

