\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\sa63\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\i0 \cf2 Strategic Considerations for Expansion\par

\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1294\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls143\ilvl0
\f2 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	1.	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Target enabling environments: Senegal, Kenya, and Benin offer the strongest ecosystem alignment for inclusive green ventures.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1301\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls143\ilvl0
\f2 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	2.	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Use donor-blended funds for entry: Many public programs prioritize youth and women but require NGO or incubator partnerships (e.g. DER, FAIEJ, YEDF).\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1291\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls143\ilvl0
\f2 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	3.	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Match sector to country: Agri-led ventures fit Senegal/Benin; digital platforms scale well in Kenya/South Africa; solar/EV mobility pilots can grow from Togo outward.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1289\sa159\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls143\ilvl0
\f2 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	4.	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Seek regional public-private accelerators: These include MCC, EnDev, GIZ, and AfDB\'92s gender-lens initiatives, many of which are pushing cross-border collaborations.
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 In summary, youth and women-led green green businesses have carved pathways in agriculture, solar energy, and recycling, but often lack funding to grow beyond early stages. Still, through donor-backed programs and rising regional demand, the barriers to expansion are falling. Strategic sector-country matching, bundled with inclusive policy instruments, can unlock real opportunities for scaling across West, East, and Southern Africa.\


\pard\tx1275\tx1735\tx1735\pardeftab720\li1735\fi-1735\sa10\partightenfactor0
\ls144\ilvl1
\f3\b \cf4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	4.2.	}
\f0 \cf4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Barriers and Enablers for Inclusive Scaling\cf2 \par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1295\sa4\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 As Africa advances toward a green economy, it is essential that women and youth are integrated not merely as passive participants but as co-creators, innovators, and founders. Across Kenya, South Africa, Senegal, Benin, and Togo, this section explores the systemic, policy, and ecosystem-level conditions shaping inclusive entrepreneurship in key green sectors, renewable energy, agriculture, digital innovation, and circular economy.\par

\pard\tx1275\tx1839\tx1839\pardeftab720\li1839\fi-1839\sa54\partightenfactor0
\ls145\ilvl2
\f6\i \cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone 	4.2.1.\cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Systemic Barriers Hindering Entrepreneurial Growth\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1995\sa59\partightenfactor0

\f0\i0\b \cf2 Limited Access to Capital and Finance\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1301\sa236\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 Access to capital remains a critical barrier. Women and youth-led ventures receive a disproportionate share of climate finance:\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\sa91\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls146\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Women-led green businesses receive just 2% of total VC in climate tech.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1302\sa244\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls146\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Youth-led green ventures face a $42 billion funding gap, with most grants targeting later-stage companies.\par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa241\qj\partightenfactor0\n\f1\b0 \cf2 Challenges include:\

\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\sa90\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls147\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Collateral requirements: 72% of women lack land/property titles\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1289\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls147\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Biased lending norms: Higher rejection rates for women and informal youth-led enterprises.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1288\sa235\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls147\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Over-centralized programs: National funds like Togo\'92s FAIEJ or Kenya\'92s Youth Enterprise Fund remain inaccessible due to bureaucracy and urban bias.\par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1500\ri1298\sa151\qj\partightenfactor0

\f6\i \cf9 Opportunity: green businesses should seek blended finance models or gender/youth-targeted grants and turn to investors from the diaspora to overcome early financing barriers.\page 
\pard\pardeftab720\li1995\sa59\partightenfactor0

\f0\i0\b \cf2 Fragmented Regulatory and Trade Ecosystems\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa236\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 Regulations are often not harmonized across African countries. For instance, solar certification from Senegal isn\'92t valid in the SADC region, hindering regional scale. Procurement systems tend to favor established, male-led enterprises, while informal businesses face licensing hurdles, especially in sectors like waste management and agritech.\par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1500\ri1292\sa240\qj\partightenfactor0

\f6\i \cf9 Action: Green businesses expanding regionally must prioritize compliance mapping and push for mutual recognition protocols within regional blocs like ECOWAS or SADC.\cf2 \par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1995\sa58\partightenfactor0

\f0\i0\b \cf2 Skills Gaps and Education Misalignment\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1289\sa237\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 Many women and youth are locked out of green tech entrepreneurship due to lack of access to STEM education and hands-on technical training. Entrepreneurial potential is sti\uc0\u64258 ed by gaps in technical, business and soft skills:\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\sa91\partightenfactor0
\ls148\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Only 12% of engineering graduates are women in Africa.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1300\partightenfactor0
\ls148\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone TVET programs often lack practical relevance or coverage in emerging green sectors.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1300\sa239\partightenfactor0
\ls148\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Young entrepreneurs in digital and agri-tech lack incubation pathways and business development training.\par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1297\sa240\qj\partightenfactor0

\f6\i \cf9 Founders should seek market-aligned technical training, mentorship, and join sector-specific accelerators (e.g. climate tech labs, circular economy incubators).\cf2 \par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1995\sa58\partightenfactor0

\f0\i0\b \cf2 Digital Exclusion and Cultural Norms\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1291\sa237\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 Green businesses in rural areas, often led by young people and women, suffer from poor internet and energy access. In sectors like clean manufacturing and renewable energy, deep-rooted gender stereotypes limit female participation. Many youth are treated as a homogenous group in policy design, neglecting nuanced needs by region and class.\par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1500\ri1301\sa240\qj\partightenfactor0

\f6\i \cf9 Action: Entrepreneurs should leverage mobile-first models (e.g. M-KOPA ) and form cooperatives to formalize operations and attract external financing.\cf2 \par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1995\sa58\partightenfactor0

\f0\i0\b \cf2 Structural Inequities and Market Barriers\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\sa155\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 Several hurdles block green businesses from scaling:\page 
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1300\partightenfactor0
\ls149\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Informality: Many women/youth-run initiatives operate informally, limiting access to procurement, credit, and legal protection.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1292\partightenfactor0
\ls149\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Digital divide: Low internet/device access hinders tech-driven models in rural zones.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1301\partightenfactor0
\ls149\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Policy inconsistency: e.g., Senegal\'92s solar certification is not recognized in SADC, creating regulatory friction for regional expansion.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1303\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls149\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Social norms: In Togo and Benin, energy and construction sectors are seen as male-dominated, discouraging female participation.\par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1288\sa3\partightenfactor0

\f6\i \cf9 Formalizing operations, leveraging digital tools like M-KOPA PAYG systems, and engaging in cross-border programs like AfCFTA can enable market access.\cf2 \par

\pard\tx1275\tx1911\pardeftab720\li1911\fi-1911\sa55\partightenfactor0
\ls150\ilvl2\cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	4.2.2.	}\cf5 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Key Enablers for Scaling Inclusive Green Ventures\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1995\sa59\partightenfactor0

\f0\i0\b \cf2 National and Donor-led Inclusion Funds\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\sa59\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 Several countries are setting up gender/youth-focused funds:\par

\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1290\partightenfactor0
\ls151\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Senegal\'92s DER/FJ has backed 20,000+ inclusive green businesses in solar and agribusiness.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1300\partightenfactor0
\ls151\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Kenya\'92s green banks and Togo\'92s FAIEJ support early-stage women/youth entrepreneurs.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1300\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls151\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone AfDB\'92s AFAWA and UNDP\'92s AGBFI channel climate capital with inclusive targets.\par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1288\sa239\partightenfactor0

\f6\i \cf9 Green businesses should monitor these facilities and apply to funds designed for their demographic and sectoral profile.\cf2 \par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1995\sa59\partightenfactor0

\f0\i0\b \cf2 Innovation Hubs and Regional Market Integration\par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\sa60\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0 \cf2 Support platforms like:\par

\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1288\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls152\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone iHub (Kenya), Jokkolabs (Senegal), and GreenCape (South Africa ) offer mentorship, prototyping, and pre-seed capital. In addition, Holocene notes that most investable founders are over 30 and often from strong university backgrounds (UCT, Stellenbosch, Wits). While women-founded companies are underfunded, Josh adds, \'93We view them as an undervalued asset. Some of our strongest teams are women-led.\'94\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1293\sa155\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls152\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone AfCFTA enables cross-border trade, lowering expansion barriers for green product and service exporters.
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1290\sa240\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls152\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Sector-specific examples include Solar Sister, enabling 8,500+ women to distribute clean energy in rural areas.\par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1500\ri1288\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f6\i \cf9 Founders should join these innovation communities and align their products with regional certification to tap into expanded markets.\cf2 \par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1995\sa54\partightenfactor0

\f0\i0\b \cf2 Inclusive Procurement and Policy Reform\par

\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1295\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls153\ilvl3
\f5\b0 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Public procurement is a powerful but underused tool: Kenya and South Africa are piloting gender-responsive budgeting and youth quotas in climate projects.\par
\pard\tx1635\tx1995\pardeftab720\li1995\fi-1995\ri1292\sa234\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls153\ilvl3
\f5 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\'81\'9c	}
\f1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone South Africa\'92s Just Transition Framework mandates youth and black-owned business inclusion.\par
\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\ri1296\sa7\qj\partightenfactor0

\f6\i \cf9 Green businesses should register for green public procurement schemes and monitor inclusive tenders under national transition programs.\cf2 \par

\pard\pardeftab720\li1275\sa3\qj\partightenfactor0

